<%#
  One row per call, newest first, which is the order the list shows them in.

  find_each is what keeps this flat in memory: a month is around 56.000 rows
  and loading them at once, four associations each, is hundreds of megabytes
  on a box with 3,7 GB. order: :desc walks the primary key backwards, and here
  that is the same order as created_at — a call's row is written when the call
  starts, so ids and start times run together.
%>
<%#
  raw on every line: ERB escapes HTML by default, and CSVSafe defuses a
  dangerous value by prefixing an apostrophe — which came out as &#39; in the
  cell. Nothing here is HTML, so there is nothing to escape.

  CSVSafe rather than CSV because a contact's name is whatever the contact
  called themselves: one starting with =, +, - or @ runs as a formula when the
  file opens in Excel.
%>
<%= raw CSVSafe.generate_line [I18n.t('reports.period', since: @period_since, until: @period_until)] -%>
<%= raw CSVSafe.generate_line [] -%>
<%= raw CSVSafe.generate_line [
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.started_at'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.kind'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.direction'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.caller_number'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.callee_number'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.contact_name'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.agent_name'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.duration_seconds'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.inbox_name'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.conversation_id'),
      I18n.t('reports.voice_calls_csv.language')
    ] -%>
<% @calls.find_each(batch_size: @batch_size, order: :desc) do |call| %>
<%#
  kind is the column the reader actually came for, and it is the one neither
  status nor direction can answer alone — an inbound call the caller abandoned
  while it rang is stored as 'completed'. Custom::Call#display_kind decides it,
  and the dashboard reads the same value, so the file and the screen cannot
  label the same call differently.

  direction is kept beside it because it is what pivots: kind already tells
  you a call was missed, direction lets you count missed against received.

  The agent is whoever took the call inbound, and whoever dialled it outbound.
  Raw seconds rather than the formatted duration on screen, for the same reason
  every other export here does it: that is what adds up in a spreadsheet.

  language is blank for any call that never went through the voice IVR's
  language menu — most calls today, since it is turned on one inbox at a
  time rather than everywhere at once. Where it is present, this column
  is what answers the two questions that decide whether the menu is worth
  keeping on: how often each language gets chosen, and — by comparing against
  the agent column — how often Custom::Voice::CallRouter's fallback fires
  because nobody online spoke the language picked.
%>
<%= raw CSVSafe.generate_line [
      local_time(call.created_at).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'),
      I18n.t("reports.voice_calls_csv.kinds.#{call.display_kind}"),
      I18n.t("reports.voice_calls_csv.directions.#{call.direction}"),
      call.from_number,
      call.to_number,
      call.contact&.name,
      call.accepted_by_agent&.available_name,
      call.duration_seconds,
      call.inbox.name,
      call.conversation&.display_id,
      call.language.present? ? I18n.t("reports.voice_calls_csv.languages.#{call.language}", default: call.language) : nil
    ] -%>
<% end %>
